<UserControl
    x:Class="ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Player.TransportControlsView"
    xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
    xmlns:automation="clr-namespace:Avalonia.Automation;assembly=Avalonia.Controls"
    xmlns:player="clr-namespace:ApSolutions.LocalMedia.Presentation.Player"
    x:DataType="player:TransportControlsViewModel"
    automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportAccessibleName}">
  <StackPanel x:Name="TransportSurface" Spacing="{DynamicResource Space8}">
    <!--
      The position, which this application did not show at all until 2026-08-22 and which the
      prototype puts across the whole width of the transport: elapsed on the left, the bar, the
      length on the right. It is the defect this repository has a name for — the model already held
      Position, Duration and SeekAsync, three values read on every state change and painted nowhere,
      so a person watching a film could not see where they were in it.

      The row is absent rather than disabled while the engine has not said how long the file is. A
      thumb halfway along a bar of unknown length points at nothing, and a greyed bar would say "not
      for you" where the truth is "not yet".
    -->
    <Grid
        x:Name="PositionSurface"
        ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto"
        ColumnSpacing="{DynamicResource Space12}"
        IsVisible="{Binding HasDuration}">
      <TextBlock
          Grid.Column="0"
          Classes="transport-clock"
          Text="{Binding PositionLabel}" />
      <!--
        Snapped to whole seconds, which is what makes the guard in OnPositionChanged a comparison
        rather than an epsilon: the engine's position arrives in fractions, the thumb sits on an
        integer, and both sides round to the same number when nobody has dragged anything.
      -->
      <Slider
          x:Name="PositionSlider"
          Grid.Column="1"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          Minimum="0"
          Maximum="{Binding DurationSeconds}"
          TickFrequency="1"
          IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportPositionLabel}"
          ValueChanged="OnPositionChanged"
          Value="{Binding PositionSeconds, Mode=OneWay}" />
      <TextBlock
          Grid.Column="2"
          Classes="transport-clock"
          Text="{Binding DurationLabel}" />
    </Grid>

    <!--
      One row of controls, which is the prototype's shape and not what this was: the volume slider
      used to sit under a label of its own on a second line, and the speed on a third. The three
      buttons wear player-chrome for its 44 px target area - a glyph needs a square target more than
      a word does - and the seconds each skip covers stay in HelpText, where a screen reader finds
      them, because the glyph cannot say "ten".

      A WrapPanel and not a horizontal StackPanel, for the reason this tree has measured nine times:
      a stack offers its children infinite width and draws them where they fall. This row carries a
      slider with a width of its own and two translated readouts, so it is exactly the shape that has
      put a control outside the window before.
    -->
    <WrapPanel
        x:Name="TransportRow"
        ItemSpacing="{DynamicResource Space8}"
        LineSpacing="{DynamicResource Space8}">
      <Button
          x:Name="SkipBackwardButton"
          Classes="player-chrome"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportSkipBackward}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Left"
          automation:AutomationProperties.HelpText="{Binding BackwardSkipLabel}"
          Command="{Binding SkipBackwardCommand}">
        <Path Classes="icon size-20" Data="{DynamicResource IconSkipBackward}" />
      </Button>
      <!--
        Play, pause and stop, between the skips, which is where the prototype puts them and where
        this application did not: they lived on a second row because their commands belong to the
        session coordinator and the skips belong to ControlPlayback, and the two models are built at
        different moments. That is a fact about the models, not about the row — the buttons reach
        across to the player's own model through the view they are hosted in, and the transport is
        one line again.
      -->
      <Button
          Classes="player-chrome"
          IsVisible="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).CanResume}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource PlayerPlayAction}"
          Command="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).ResumeCommand}">
        <Path Classes="icon size-22 filled" Data="{DynamicResource IconPlay}" />
      </Button>
      <Button
          Classes="player-chrome"
          IsVisible="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).CanPause}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource PlayerPauseAction}"
          Command="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).PauseCommand}">
        <Path Classes="icon size-22" Data="{DynamicResource IconPause}" />
      </Button>
      <Button
          x:Name="SkipForwardButton"
          Classes="player-chrome"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportSkipForward}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Right"
          automation:AutomationProperties.HelpText="{Binding ForwardSkipLabel}"
          Command="{Binding SkipForwardCommand}">
        <Path Classes="icon size-20" Data="{DynamicResource IconSkipForward}" />
      </Button>
      <Button
          Classes="player-chrome"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource PlayerStopAction}"
          Command="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).StopCommand}">
        <Path Classes="icon size-22" Data="{DynamicResource IconStop}" />
      </Button>
      <Button
          x:Name="MuteButton"
          Classes="player-chrome"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportToggleMute}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="M"
          Command="{Binding ToggleMuteCommand}">
        <!--
          Two shapes and not one: the prototype swaps its speaker for a crossed speaker the moment
          the session is muted, and this button was drawing the crossed one in both states — a
          control that says the same thing whatever it has done.
        -->
        <Panel>
          <Path Classes="icon size-18" Data="{DynamicResource IconVolume}" IsVisible="{Binding !IsMuted}" />
          <Path Classes="icon size-18" Data="{DynamicResource IconMute}" IsVisible="{Binding IsMuted}" />
        </Panel>
      </Button>
      <!--
        The binding stays one way on purpose: VolumePercent is what the session came back with, and
        a two-way binding would write the thumb's position into a property that has no setter. What
        carries a chosen level to the session is the handler, which is what this control did not have
        until 2026-08-15 - it moved, and nothing heard it.

        132 is the prototype's width and it has no token: the three scales this repository gates are
        the type sizes, the five spaces and the two radii, and the width of one slider is none of
        them. Without a width a slider inside a WrapPanel measures to its own tiny default.
      -->
      <Slider
          x:Name="VolumeSlider"
          Width="132"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          Minimum="{x:Static player:TransportControlsViewModel.MinimumVolumePercent}"
          Maximum="{x:Static player:TransportControlsViewModel.MaximumVolumePercent}"
          TickFrequency="5"
          IsSnapToTickEnabled="True"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportVolumeLabel}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Up"
          ValueChanged="OnVolumeChanged"
          Value="{Binding VolumePercent, Mode=OneWay}" />
      <!--
        The level as a number, which the slider alone never said. It is the same omission the
        position was: VolumePercent existed, the thumb moved, and nothing on screen told anybody
        whether they were at 80 or at 180 - which matters here, because above 100 the limiter comes
        on and the warning below explains why.
      -->
      <TextBlock
          x:Name="VolumeReadout"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          Foreground="{DynamicResource PlayerTextBrush}"
          Text="{Binding VolumeLabel}" />
      <!--
        The prototype's speed menu, which this drew as eleven bare numbers in a MenuFlyout: the
        pill wears SPEED and the current multiplier, the nine steps behind it carry a mark, a name
        and a note, and «Volver a 1×» is a button of its own beside the pill rather than an
        eleventh row — a row that resets is not a speed, and it was sitting in a list of speeds.

        A ComboBox and not a button with a flyout, and that is the walk rather than taste. Nothing
        inside a Flyout can be pressed by the harness — a flyout's content lands in a popup root of
        its own, which is why all twenty entries of eng/walk-pending.txt are flyout children and why
        that ratchet cannot take two more. A ComboBox is pressed and asserted on IsDropDownOpen, the
        way the library's two filters already are, and its rows are ComboBoxItems, which the
        inventory does not count as separate command controls. So the menu gains a shape and the
        ledger gains exactly one identity: the reset beside it.

        filter-pill is the prototype's drop-down and already draws all of this — the label in small
        type, the value in semi-bold, the caret that turns over when the panel opens, and the accent
        frame while it is open. speed-pill adds the two things only this one wants: it opens upward,
        and its closed face says the multiplier where the row says «Normal».
      -->
      <ComboBox
          x:Name="SpeedReadout"
          Classes="filter-pill speed-pill"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          Tag="{DynamicResource TransportSpeedShortLabel}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportSpeedLabel}"
          ItemsSource="{Binding SpeedOptions}"
          SelectedValue="{Binding SpeedMultiplier, Mode=OneWay}"
          SelectedValueBinding="{Binding Multiplier}"
          SelectionChanged="OnSpeedChanged">
        <ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
          <DataTemplate x:DataType="player:SpeedOption">
            <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="{DynamicResource Space8}">
              <!--
                The mark, which is the prototype's own «●» and the third signal this row carries.
                It is not decoration beside the wash and the border: both high contrast dictionaries
                resolve the selected fill and the resting fill to one colour, so on those two themes
                the mark is the only thing left that says which speed is in force — the same reason
                the appearance pills carry a state glyph and the navigation rail carries a bar.

                Hidden here and shown by ComboBoxItem:selected, so the row that has it is the row the
                control itself calls selected. A bound IsCurrent would be a second opinion about the
                same fact, and the two would disagree the first time one of them was not refreshed.
              -->
              <TextBlock
                  Classes="speed-mark"
                  Width="14"
                  TextAlignment="Center"
                  Text="●" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="1" Text="{Binding Label}" />
              <TextBlock Grid.Column="2" Classes="setting-state" Text="{Binding Note}" />
            </Grid>
          </DataTemplate>
        </ComboBox.ItemTemplate>
      </ComboBox>
      <!--
        Present only while there is something to come back from, which is the same grammar the
        library's «Limpiar filtros» follows and the prototype's own rateNotNormal. A reset
        permanently on offer is a control whose job would be nothing.
      -->
      <Button
          x:Name="SpeedResetButton"
          VerticalAlignment="Center"
          IsVisible="{Binding IsAwayFromNormalSpeed}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportSpeedResetAction}"
          Command="{Binding SetSpeedCommand}"
          CommandParameter="1"
          Content="{DynamicResource TransportSpeedResetAction}" />
      <!--
        Picture-in-picture and full screen, at the end of the bar, which is where every player on
        this machine puts them and where this one did not have them at all: the two modes were
        reachable from the header pill row above the picture and from the keyboard, and the owner
        looked for them in the transport — «no hay botón de pantalla completa en la barra de
        controles», «el icono de PiP debe ir en la barra de controles, junto al de pantalla
        completa».

        They reach the player's own model rather than the shell's, and that is not a preference: this
        very control is handed to the mini window, where there is no shell above it to bind up to.
      -->
      <Button
          x:Name="PictureInPictureButton"
          Classes="player-chrome"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportPictureInPictureAction}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="Ctrl+P"
          Command="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).TogglePictureInPictureCommand}">
        <Path Classes="icon size-18" Data="{DynamicResource IconMiniPlayer}" />
      </Button>
      <Button
          x:Name="FullscreenButton"
          Classes="player-chrome"
          automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportFullscreenAction}"
          automation:AutomationProperties.AcceleratorKey="F"
          Command="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).ToggleFullscreenCommand}">
        <!--
          Two shapes and not one, which is the same correction the mute button took on 2026-08-25 and
          for the same reason: the prototype writes
          `icon(mode === 'fullscreen' ? 'exitfull' : 'full')` and this drew the entering arrows in
          both states — a control that says the same thing whatever it has done. `IconExitFullscreen`
          was in the dictionary the whole time and only the mini window's restore ever asked for it.
        -->
        <Panel>
          <Path
              Classes="icon size-18"
              Data="{DynamicResource IconFullscreen}"
              IsVisible="{Binding !$parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).IsFullscreen}" />
          <Path
              Classes="icon size-18"
              Data="{DynamicResource IconExitFullscreen}"
              IsVisible="{Binding $parent[player:PlayerView].((player:PlayerViewModel)DataContext).IsFullscreen}" />
        </Panel>
      </Button>
    </WrapPanel>

    <Border
        x:Name="BoostWarningSurface"
        IsVisible="{Binding RequiresBoostWarning}"
        Padding="8"
        Background="{DynamicResource AccentSubtleBrush}"
        BorderBrush="{DynamicResource ShellHairlineBrush}"
        BorderThickness="1"
        CornerRadius="{DynamicResource CornerRadiusMedium}"
        automation:AutomationProperties.Name="{DynamicResource TransportBoostWarningTitle}">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" Spacing="{DynamicResource Space8}">
        <TextBlock
            x:Name="BoostWarningIcon"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            FontWeight="Bold"
            Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
            Text="!" />
        <TextBlock
            x:Name="BoostWarningText"
            VerticalAlignment="Center"
            Foreground="{DynamicResource TextPrimaryBrush}"
            Text="{DynamicResource TransportBoostWarningText}"
            TextWrapping="Wrap" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
